Even at this coarse resolution the simulated moments match the analytic stationary values to within about a percent. This is a numerical check of the theory: the Wiener process was the only randomness in the program, and out of it came the Gaussian equilibrium predicted by the analytics. Notice, though, that the program integrates the SDE, not the density equation. The comment in the original scratch version of this code called the Euler-Maruyama update a “Fokker-Planck update step”, which is a common slip: the particles carry the density, but the equation they satisfy collectively is a different one.

Overview
Ciw uses an event scheduling approach composed of three types of events: A, B, and C. A-type events move the system’s clock forward in time. B-type events have been pre-scheduled. C-type events are events triggered by B-type events.3{:target=“_blank”}
Ciw simulations are “time-to-event”, meaning that time between events are skipped over in the simulation.4{:target=“_blank”} This approach becomes more efficient relative to simulating the state for every time step as the linear density of events over time decreases. Ciw uses an arbitrary scale of time so it is the responsibility of the user to decide whether the base unit of the simulation is in seconds, minutes, hours, days, years or some other unit.
The core Python classes in Ciw include Simulation, ArrivalNode, ExitNode, Server, and Individual.5{:target=“_blank”} The simulation object is responsible for running the simulation, running a state tracker, and recording a log. The arrival node is responsible for generating individuals as the unit that flows through the network. Individuals either remain in the network, or they go to a terminating state called the “exit node”. Servers are the units of capacity that limit how many individuals can be served at a node in parallel.
There are also some abstractions in Ciw oriented around the topic of input and a queue representation. These include Network, ServiceCentre, CustomerClasses, and Distributions.5{:target=“_blank”}
Two optional components of Ciw are the abstractions of StateTracker and DeadlockDetector.5{:target=“_blank”} The state trackers are responsible for collecting aggregate information of the state of the simulation.6{:target=“_blank”} An example is system population tracker, which records the overall number of individuals in the queueing network for each time when an event occurs. The deadlock detector is for Type I blocking,3{:target=“_blank”} which is one of three types of blocking.7{:target=“_blank”},8{:target=“_blank”}
Although it does not have an explicitly recreational scope, Ciw has been used for recreation in the form of simulating an asynchronous variant of snakes and ladders.9{:target=“_blank”}
Ciw v3.1.0 requires Python 3.7 or greater.10{:target=“_blank”} The project has had 15-17 contributors as of December 18th, 2023.11{:target=“_blank”},12{:target=“_blank”}

Miscellaneous
Some of the miscellaneous features of Ciw include being able to set a progress bar to display the progress of a simulation44{:target=“_blank”} and set numerical calculations to be exact.45{:target=“_blank”} Extended Behaviour
There is some additional behaviour that can be obtained from Ciw that is not an officially supported feature.

Ciw can be hybridized with other forms of time-dependent processes including differential equations even if it is not a built-in feature.46,47,48{:target=“_blank”}
Ciw does not have a built-in feature for initializing the state of the system, however the HCiw package provides this functionality.49{:target=“_blank”}
There are no utilities in Ciw to schedule non-queueing functions, but this is possible with external code.50{:target=“_blank”}
Parallel computing is not a built-in feature but can be utilized for repeated runs of the simulation under differing input or pseudorandom seed.51{:target=“_blank”}
While Ciw collects complete records, it does not automatically report incomplete records (i.e. those that are still in a node at the end of the simulation). It is possible to further extract that information.52{:target=“_blank”}

Disappointed by ChatGPT in Asking ChatGPT to Code a Plotting Function for Covering Diagrams but inspired by some of the diagrams in The Joy of Abstraction: An Exploration of Math, Category Theory, and Life by Eugenia Cheng, I decided to take a quick attempt at plotting Hasse diagrams for the factorization of positive integers.
from collections import Counter
from functools import reduce
from typing import Dict, Set

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import networkx as nx
import numpy as np


def factors(n: int) -&gt; Set[int]:
    """Compute the factors of a given number."""
    return set(
        reduce(
            list.__add__,
            ([i, n // i] for i in range(1, int(n**0.5) + 1) if n % i == 0),
        )
    )


def make_graph(x: int) -&gt; nx.DiGraph:
    """Create a directed graph based on the factors of a given number."""
    nodes = factors(x)
    g = nx.DiGraph()
    for ni in nodes:
        for nj in nodes:
            if ni != nj:
                if not ni % nj:
                    g.add_edge(nj, ni)
    g = nx.transitive_reduction(g)
    return g


def pos_hasse(g: nx.DiGraph) -&gt; Dict[int, np.ndarray]:
    """Compute the positions of nodes in a Hasse diagram based on their grades."""
    grades = nx.shortest_path_length(g, source=1)
    grade_counts = Counter(grades.values())

    pos = {}

    for grade in set(grades.values()):
        gnodes = [node for node in grades if grades[node] == grade]
        gnodes = sorted(gnodes)
        center = len(gnodes) / 2
        for i, ni in enumerate(gnodes):
            pos[ni] = np.array([i - center, grades[ni]])

    return pos


def plot_hasse(n: int = 2018, output_path: str = "2023-06-10-hasse-diagram.png") -&gt; str:
    """Plot Hasse diagram for number n and save to output_path."""
    graph = make_graph(n)
    pos = pos_hasse(graph)
    nx.draw(graph, pos=pos, with_labels=True)
    plt.savefig(output_path, dpi=300, transparent=True)
    plt.close()
    return output_path


def main() -&gt; None:
    plot_hasse()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
